const intro={"n":6,"title":"An Epidemic, an Act of Gratitude, and the Death of Sergeant Cruz","nums":[526,543,548],"summary":"Fierro recalls an epidemic of fever and smallpox in the Indigenous settlement. He describes suffering, fear and violent responses to illness, as well as the killing of a foreign captive accused of bringing disease. Fierro and Cruz come to the aid of the Indigenous man who had originally protected them, defend him against an attempted killing and care for him until his death. Cruz subsequently falls ill. Dying, he asks Fierro to find his abandoned son if he ever returns home. Fierro is overcome when his companion dies.","people":"Martín Fierro as narrator; Sergeant Cruz; the Indigenous man who helped the two companions; a foreign-born captive accused of spreading disease; Cruz's absent son.","themes":"Epidemic and mortality; gratitude across social divisions; fear and scapegoating; friendship and care; death and a father's final request; narrator bias.","words":"virgüela (gauchesque spelling of viruela, smallpox); peste (epidemic or pestilence); gualicho (harmful magic or spell in this context); toldos (shelters or settlement); cautivo (captive); paisano (countryman).","questions":["What act of help does Fierro attribute to the Indigenous man before the epidemic?","Why do Fierro and Cruz decide to protect and care for him?","What accusation is directed against the foreign captive, and what happens to him?","What request does Cruz make for his son before he dies?","Where does the canto complicate Fierro's other hostile generalizations about Indigenous people?"],"analysis":"The man who saves and visits Fierro and Cruz is portrayed as generous; his actions complicate the poem's repeated blanket hostility toward Indigenous people. At the same time, Fierro relates frightening practices amid an epidemic and a killing driven by scapegoating. These are narrated incidents, not evidence that an entire people shared the same medical practices. Cruz's final request introduces the importance of his son for the later plot.","related":"/en/martin-fierro-advice-to-his-sons/","relatedTitle":"Fierro’s later advice to the young men"};
